const e={note:`Nine currents, and one difficulty running under all of them: this is the literature of a living community of perhaps sixty to a hundred thousand people, presently scattered and under pressure, and not a closed corpus of late antiquity. Everything below is in the present tense where the practice is present, and nothing below is instruction — the rites are described as the ethnographers and the editors describe them, from outside, and the operative material is not reproduced. Five particular cautions. Much of what is quoted here comes from naṣuraiia scrolls, priestly texts that were never meant for general circulation and that reached Oxford because Lady Drower commissioned copies of them; the access is itself part of the subject, and the community's view of it is not uniform. Transliteration follows Drower and Macuch, which writes i for consonantal y and u for w, so iardna and Hiia rather than yardna and Hayyi; where the community's own pronunciation differs — Hayyi Rabbi for Hiia Rba — that is noted rather than corrected. No Mandaean manuscript older than the sixteenth century survives, so every date assigned to a text rests on internal evidence or on the colophons, and the colophons are exactly what the current argument about Mandaean origins is being fought over; where a dating is contested it is given twice, with the parties named. "Gnostic" is an outside category, useful for comparison and not a word the tradition uses of itself. And the texts contain hard polemic against Jews, Christians and Muslims, which is reported here because it is in them; it is not adjudicated, and it is not a description of how Mandaeans live beside their neighbours now.`,themes:[{slug:"abagada",title:"The Alphabet",native:"ࡀࡁࡀࡂࡀࡃࡀ",translit:"abagada",summary:"Twenty-two letters, a ligature, and the first letter written again at the end, so that the sequence closes where it opened — and the whole of it held to be a power rather than a notation.",paragraphs:["The Mandaic alphabet runs in the ordinary Aramaic order, alaph through taw, and then does two things no other Semitic script does. The twenty-third sign is a ligature, adu, a d joined to a u, which carries the relative particle ḏ- and is counted as a letter in its own right rather than as a scribal convenience. The twenty-fourth is the first letter written a second time. The count is therefore twenty-two consonants and twenty-four members, and the tradition insists on the closing repetition: an alphabet that stops at taw is, for the purposes to which the alphabet is put, incomplete. The letters are not named after objects. There is no ox and no house here; they are called a, ba, ga, da, and the alphabet takes its name from the first four of them — abagada, or in the shorter form abaga. Beside that, Mandaic writes all its vowels with full letters and needs no pointing, which is why Mandaean texts can be voiced with a confidence impossible for unpointed Syriac or Hebrew.","Each letter is said to be a power of Life and Light, and the letters are held to impart raze, mysteries. The twenty-four are reckoned against the twenty-four hours from sunset to sunset; the first and last, being the same sign, are read as the completion of a circuit rather than as a beginning and an end. Two cautions belong here. First, most of these statements reach us in Drower's summaries of what priests told her in the 1930s, set beside the priestly commentaries she was having copied at the same time, and the two layers should not be flattened into a single ancient doctrine. Second, it is sometimes said in secondary accounts that the letters are called by a word meaning faces or images; the designations actually attested for the alphabet in the edited Mandaic texts are abagada and abaga, and the stronger claim should be treated as unsourced until someone produces the passage.","The alphabet is expounded at length in the priestly literature. Alma Rišaia Rba, the Great First World, is an illustrated scroll used in the ordination of a priest; the Drower copy, DC 41, was made in 1224 AH, that is 1809 or 1810, and it carries drawings of heavenly trees, of concentric circles marking the sacramental garments and the parts of the body, and — the item that matters most here — a figure of Adam Kasia, the hidden Adam, labelled with the letters of the alphabet. The letters are laid on a body. The Alf Trisar Šuialia, the Thousand and Twelve Questions, works through the same material catechetically, as a master answering a pupil. The resemblance to Jewish letter-mysticism, where the letters of the alphabet are distributed through the making of the world, is real and worth stating; it is a comparison between two neighbouring traditions in sibling dialects of one language, and neither Drower nor anyone since has demonstrated a derivation in either direction.","Writing in this script is itself an act with effect. Magical scrolls conventionally open by setting out the alphabet in order, often with its vowel modifications as well, before any operative text begins; the verb abaga, formed from the alphabet's name, means to recite the letters and hence to say a charm; and Drower records that the twenty-four letters, inscribed on twenty-four scraps of silver or gold, are placed under the pillow of a person who wants guidance in a difficulty. Reading the letters aloud is itself protective. None of this is presented in the sources as an application of the alphabet to some further end. The letters are not a code for the powers; they are the powers in a form that can be written down, which is why the copying of a manuscript is a religious act and why the colophons treat the labour of copying as meritorious.","The script has a history as well as a doctrine, and the two pull in different directions. Charles Häberl's study of its origins places Mandaic among the Aramaic chancery hands of the Parthian period, in a group with Elymaic and Characenean — the scripts of southern Mesopotamia and Khuzestan, the region the Mandaeans have inhabited for as long as anyone can document them. Mandaic is the most conservative member of that group: more heavily ligatured than Elymaic or Characenean, less cursive than Book Pahlavi. That is an argument about where the community was when it began to write, and it belongs to the question of origins taken up further down this hall. The script has never fallen out of use. It is taught in diaspora classes, it was encoded in Unicode in 2010, and it is at present written by more people in Sweden, Australia and the United States than in Iraq."],terms:[{native:"ࡀࡁࡀࡂࡀࡃࡀ",translit:"abagada",gloss:"the alphabet, named from its first four signs a-ba-ga-da; also abaga, which as a verb means to recite the letters and so to say a charm"},{native:"ࡀ",translit:"halqa",gloss:"the first letter, a small circle, and the twenty-fourth as well — the sequence closes by writing it again"},{native:"ࡖ",translit:"adu",gloss:"the twenty-third sign, a ligature of d and u carrying the relative particle ḏ-; counted as a letter, which is how twenty-two consonants make an alphabet of twenty-four"},{native:"ࡓࡀࡆࡀ",translit:"raza",gloss:"mystery; what the letters are said to impart, and the same word used of the rites"},{native:"ࡎࡉࡃࡓࡀ",translit:"sidra",gloss:"book, order; the Ginza is also called Sidra Rba, and the core of the prayerbook is the Sidra ḏ-Nišmata, the Book of Souls"},{native:"ࡐࡉࡔࡓࡀ",translit:"pišra",gloss:"a loosing or untying; the name of a class of exorcism text, which conventionally opens by writing the alphabet out in order"}],texts:[{title:"Alma Rišaia Rba (DC 41)",note:"An illustrated priestly scroll used at ordination, the Drower copy dated 1224 AH / 1809–10; it carries the figure of Adam Kasia labelled with the letters. Translated by E. S. Drower in A Pair of Naṣoraean Commentaries (1963)."},{title:"Alf Trisar Šuialia (The Thousand and Twelve Questions)",note:"The priestly catechism, in question-and-answer form; translated by Drower (1960) from DC 6. The fullest single source for what priests are taught about the letters, the rites and the soul."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)",note:"The source for the alphabet's ordinary uses — the twenty-four scraps of silver or gold, the reading aloud as protection, the verb abaga."},{title:"Charles G. Häberl, 'Iranian Scripts for Aramaic Languages: The Origin of the Mandaic Script', BASOR 341 (2006), 53–62",note:"Places Mandaic with Elymaic and Characenean among the Parthian-period chancery hands, and makes the script itself evidence in the argument about origins."},{title:"E. S. Drower and Rudolf Macuch, A Mandaic Dictionary (1963)",note:"The standard lexicon, and the source of the transliteration conventions followed throughout this hall."}]},{slug:"the-two-worlds",title:"The Two Worlds",native:"ࡀࡋࡌࡀ ࡖࡍࡄࡅࡓࡀ",translit:"alma ḏ-nhura",summary:"A World of Light of living water and radiant beings, a World of Darkness below the earth, and a boundary at which a reflection in black water becomes a maker of worlds.",paragraphs:["At the head of everything stands the Life — Hiia, in the Drower and Macuch transliteration; Hayyi as the community says it — addressed with a plural form and a singular sense, and titled Hiia Rba, the Great Life. The Life is in the alma ḏ-nhura, the World of Light, which the texts describe in terms of light, of fragrance, and above all of living water: the celestial Jordan is the model of which every earthly river is a copy, not the other way round. The World of Light is populated by uthras, beings of light whose name Drower renders as ethereal being; each has a škinta, a dwelling. Below the first Life the texts range further Lives — Yušamin the Second, Abatur the Third, Ptahil the Fourth — in a descending series in which each is further from the source and correspondingly less secure, so that the disorder in the cosmos begins not at the bottom but a little way down from the top.","The World of Darkness, alma ḏ-hšuka, lies beneath Tibil, the earth. It is a realm of black water and of a great dark sea; the Ginza speaks of the Abaddons, upper and lower, and of Siniawis; the river Hitfun divides it from what is above. Its king is Ur, described in dragon or leviathan terms, and its queen is Ruha, mother of the seven planets and the twelve signs. Beneath or behind them the texts set Krun, styled a great mountain of flesh, the oldest and lowest of the powers of darkness. The dualism is not symmetrical, and this matters for placing Mandaeism beside its neighbours: the Life is prior, the darkness is not its equal and opposite, and no Mandaean text sets two eternal principles against each other in the Zoroastrian manner, however much Iranian vocabulary the tradition has absorbed.","The most consequential episode in the whole cosmology happens at the boundary between the two. Abatur, the Third Life, is stationed at the frontier; he looks down into the black water and sees his own image there, and from that image Ptahil arises and goes down to make the world. Creation, on this account, is not an act of will but a reflection — and a reflection into darkness. Abatur is thereafter the weigher of souls, seated at the head of the road the dead must travel, with the scales before him; in the illustrated scroll that bears his name he is drawn there, his face covered. That the maker of the world should be the mirror-child of the judge of souls is the sort of arrangement the tradition states without explaining, and both Lidzbarski and Rudolph, translating and reconstructing a century apart, leave it standing.","Set against the mirror in the black water is the doctrine of the dmuta, the image or counterpart. Every soul has one in the World of Light; the ritual objects have them; there is a whole world of them, Mšunia Kušṭa, the world of ideal counterparts, where what is here below exists in its true form. The purpose of the death-liturgy is to bring the soul back into union with its dmuta. This is a doctrine of doubles held in a tradition that also teaches that the world was made from a double, and the tension is productive rather than incoherent: a likeness cast downward makes a defective world, a likeness held above keeps the soul's true form safe until it can be rejoined.","One difference from the Gnostic systems this cosmology is usually compared with should be stated plainly, because it is constantly got wrong. Mandaean texts speak of the body as a prison and of the world as the work of a lesser maker, and they nonetheless command marriage and the having of children; celibacy is not a virtue in this tradition and the priesthood is married. Fasting from food is not required, and the texts redefine fasting as abstention from lying, theft and killing. Circumcision is forbidden. The ethical programme is closer to that of a village religion than to that of a world-renouncing sect, and any account that reads Mandaeism as ascetic Gnosticism with a river attached has missed what the community actually does."],terms:[{native:"ࡀࡋࡌࡀ ࡖࡍࡄࡅࡓࡀ",translit:"alma ḏ-nhura",gloss:"the World of Light; realm of the Life, of living water and of the uthras"},{native:"ࡀࡋࡌࡀ ࡖࡄࡔࡅࡊࡀ",translit:"alma ḏ-hšuka",gloss:"the World of Darkness, below Tibil the earth, with its black waters and its king and queen"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡍࡀ",translit:"mana",gloss:"vessel, garment, mind; used of the supreme Life as the Great Mana and of the soul as a vessel of light"},{native:"ࡏࡅࡕࡓࡀ",translit:"ʿutra",gloss:"a being of the World of Light. Etymology disputed: from ʿutra, riches, or on Häberl's argument from a root meaning to exceed, hence excellency"},{native:"ࡔࡊࡉࡍࡕࡀ",translit:"škinta",gloss:"dwelling; the celestial house of an uthra, and also a ritual enclosure on earth. Cognate with Hebrew šekinah, but here a place rather than a presence"},{native:"ࡃࡌࡅࡕࡀ",translit:"dmuta",gloss:"image, counterpart; the heavenly double with which the soul is to be reunited"}],texts:[{title:"Ginza Rba, Right Ginza",note:"Eighteen tractates of cosmology, myth and ethics. Petermann's lithographic edition of the Mandaic appeared in 1867; Lidzbarski's German translation of 1925 is still the version most scholarship works from."},{title:"Diwan Abatur (DC 8)",note:"The illustrated scroll of the road of the dead, drawing Abatur at the boundary with his scales; published by Drower in 1950 under the title Diwan Abatur, or Progress through the Purgatories — the Latin word is hers, not the tradition's."},{title:"Kurt Rudolph, Die Mandäer I: Prolegomena; II: Der Kult (1960–61)",note:"The standard modern reconstruction of the doctrine and of the ritual, and the work against which later accounts still position themselves."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Secret Adam: A Study of Nasoraean Gnosis (1960)",note:"Her synthesis of the priestly material, on Adam Kasia and the doctrine of counterparts; suggestive, and to be read knowing how much of it rests on scrolls she alone had seen."},{title:"Carlos Gelbert, Ginza Rba (2011)",note:"A complete English rendering of the Great Treasure by a Mandaean scholar; the first in the language, and useful beside Lidzbarski rather than instead of him."}]},{slug:"the-descents",title:"The Descents",native:"ࡄࡉࡁࡉࡋ ࡆࡉࡅࡀ",translit:"Hibil Ziwa",summary:"The World of Light sends messengers downward; one of them comes back needing to be washed, and the maker of the world is promised forgiveness.",paragraphs:["The structural move of Mandaean myth is descent. From the Life proceeds Manda ḏ-Hiia, the Knowledge of Life, who goes down and calls; beside and sometimes indistinguishable from him stand Hibil Ziwa, Šitil and Anuš, and around them a crowd of further uthras with overlapping functions. The overlap is not carelessness on the part of the redactors but a feature the texts are comfortable with: figures merge, exchange speeches and take one another's titles, and a scholar's attempt to build a clean genealogy of redeemers usually says more about the scholar than about the corpus. Rudolph's reconstruction distinguishes strata within the material; Buckley reads the fluidity as itself doctrinal, on the ground that a being of light is a mode of the Life rather than an individual in the ordinary sense. Neither reading has driven out the other.","The fullest descent narrative is the Diwan of the Baptism of Hibil Ziwa, which Drower published in 1953 from DC 35 alongside the Haran Gawaita. Hibil goes down through the worlds of darkness one after another, taking their mysteries as he goes, and seals Ruha; and on his return he has to be baptised, because he has been where he has been. That detail carries the weight of the whole system. Purity in this tradition is not a metaphor for moral standing and contamination is not merely a figure for sin; contact has consequences that water addresses, and a being of light who descends acquires the same liability as anyone else. The Mandaean redeemer does not pass through the lower world untouched.","The making of the world is a failure, and the texts say so without embarrassment. Ptahil, the Fourth Life, son of Abatur, goes down to fashion Tibil, and the work goes wrong; Ruha's part in it and Ptahil's vary from telling to telling, each gaining as the other's power slackens. The body of Adam is made and cannot stand: it lies inert until the nišimta, the soul, is brought down from the World of Light and placed in it, and only then does the human being rise. Ptahil is afterwards detained and punished for what he made. But the Mandaean demiurge is not damned. The texts hold out his forgiveness at the end of the ages, which sets this tradition apart from the Sethian and Valentinian systems it is habitually grouped with, where the maker of the world is a permanent error.","Adam's three sons are Hibil, Šitil and Anuš — Abel, Seth and Enosh in the biblical roll, but here uthras who bear those names rather than the persons of Genesis. Šitil's soul is the tradition's model of a pure soul, which is why one whole class of death-liturgy is named after him. Anuš appears in the Book of John as a figure who comes to Jerusalem. Behind and above the man of flesh, Adam Pagria, stands Adam Kasia, the hidden Adam, his celestial counterpart; the priestly scrolls map the letters of the alphabet onto his body, and the priest at his ordination is dressed and addressed in terms that assimilate him to the archetype. The relation between the two Adams is the relation between every person and their dmuta, stated once at the top of the human race.","The polemic that runs through this material has to be reported and then set down carefully. In the Draša ḏ-Iahia, the Book of John — seventy-six chapters in Lidzbarski’s division, which is the standard reckoning, edited by Lidzbarski in 1905 and 1915 and given a critical edition, translation and commentary by Charles Häberl and James McGrath in 2020 — John the Baptist is the great baptiser and the honoured teacher, and Jesus is presented as one who received the baptism and altered it. There is a long and vivid sequence about Miriai, a girl of a Jewish family who leaves her people for the new teaching and is cursed by them; there is the discourse of the Good Shepherd, and there is the Fisher of Souls. These are the texts of a small community defining itself against much larger neighbours in late antiquity, and they are quoted here because they are what the books say. They are not an account of how Mandaeans and their Christian, Jewish and Muslim neighbours live beside one another now, and they should not be used as one."],terms:[{native:"ࡄࡉࡁࡉࡋ ࡆࡉࡅࡀ",translit:"Hibil Ziwa",gloss:"Hibil the Radiance; the uthra who descends into the World of Darkness and must be baptised on his return"},{native:"ࡉࡅࡔࡀࡌࡉࡍ",translit:"Yušamin",gloss:"the Second Life, an uthra who overreaches and is set aside — the tradition's account of disorder beginning above rather than below"},{native:"ࡀࡁࡀࡕࡅࡓ",translit:"Abatur",gloss:"the Third Life; stationed at the boundary of the two worlds, weigher of souls, his face veiled"},{native:"ࡐࡕࡀࡄࡉࡋ",translit:"Ptahil",gloss:"the Fourth Life; the fashioner of the material world, whose work fails, who is punished, and who is nevertheless promised forgiveness"},{native:"ࡀࡃࡀࡌ ࡊࡀࡎࡉࡀ",translit:"Adam Kasia",gloss:"the hidden Adam, celestial counterpart of Adam Pagria, the Adam of the body"},{native:"ࡔࡉࡕࡉࡋ",translit:"Šitil",gloss:"Seth; the son whose soul is the pattern of a pure soul, and after whom one class of death-liturgy is named"}],texts:[{title:"Diwan of the Baptism of Hibil Ziwa (DC 35)",note:"The descent into the World of Darkness and the washing that follows it; translated by Drower in 1953, bound with the Haran Gawaita in the same volume."},{title:"Ginza Rba, Right Ginza III and V",note:"The making of the world and of Adam, Abatur at the boundary, and Ptahil's failure; in Lidzbarski's translation of 1925."},{title:"Charles G. Häberl and James F. McGrath, The Mandaean Book of John: Critical Edition, Translation, and Commentary (2020)",note:"The Draša ḏ-Iahia in a modern critical edition prepared in consultation with the community, and published open access."},{title:"Mark Lidzbarski, Das Johannesbuch der Mandäer (1905, 1915)",note:"Text in the first part, German translation in the second; the edition on which a century of argument about John the Baptist in this literature was conducted."},{title:"Jorunn Jacobsen Buckley, The Mandaeans: Ancient Texts and Modern People (2002)",note:"A study of the figures and the rites by a scholar who worked with the community; the best single entry to the mythology for a reader coming from outside."}]},{slug:"masbuta",title:"The River",native:"ࡌࡉࡀ ࡄࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"mia hiia",summary:"A baptism that is repeated rather than entered once, that can only be performed in moving water, and that has become in the last thirty years a question of hydrology.",paragraphs:["The maṣbuta requires flowing water and nothing else will serve. A cistern will not do, a font will not do, and a pool will not do unless it is fed by a current; the water must be mia hiia, living water, and any river that qualifies is called a iardna, a Jordan. The rite is performed by a priest, ordinarily on habšaba, the first day of the week, and it is repeated — after childbirth, at a marriage, at the great festivals, after any defilement, at intervals through an ordinary year. This is the point most consistently misunderstood by outsiders, and the misunderstanding has a documented history: the Portuguese and Carmelite missionaries who met the community at Basra in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries called them the Christians of St John and took the repeated washing for a wayward version of a rite that in their own tradition happened once.","The sequence is well documented, and it is set out here as the descriptions record it. The priest, in the white rasta, wearing the klila of myrtle, with the margna of olive wood in his hand and the drabša planted at the bank, leads the candidate into the water. Water is thrown over them while the malwaša, the ritual name, is pronounced; there is a first kušṭa, the clasping of right hands; the head is immersed three times; the forehead is signed three times with water drawn across it from right to left; water is drunk three times from the priest's palm; the myrtle wreath is taken from the priest's own head and set in the candidate's burzinqa, the ritual turban. On the bank the forehead is anointed with sesame oil, and the rite closes with the pihta, the bread, and the mambuha, the draught of water, and a final kušṭa.","The tradition distinguishes carefully between what a person may do alone and what requires the priesthood, and only the last of the three is a maṣbuta. The rišama is the daily ablution before prayer, performed alone. The ṭamaša is a threefold immersion a person performs alone after bodily pollution or an unclean thought. The maṣbuta needs a priest, the ritual dress, the bread and the water, the banner and the myrtle, and a river. Reading these three as a single scale of intensity is a common error; they are three different things, and the vocabulary keeps them apart. What they share is the assumption that the ordinary condition of a body in the world is one from which water is the way back.","The rite also stands at the end of a life. The dead are washed, dressed in the rasta and buried without delay, oriented to the north, which is the direction of the World of Light and the direction in which Mandaeans pray. The five intercalary days of Parwanaia — five epagomenal days appended to the eighth month, Šumbulta, in a calendar of twelve months of thirty days — are the season in which the greatest concentration of baptisms and of death-liturgies takes place, and the Ṭabahata masiqta, the liturgy of the ancestors, can be held at no other time in the year. A calendar of 360 days plus five is Egyptian in shape and Babylonian in its month-names, and the Mandaean year carries both without apparent difficulty.","In the last three decades this has stopped being only a matter of liturgy. The draining of the southern Iraqi marshes in the early 1990s destroyed the landscape the rite presupposes; the marshes were partly reflooded after 2003 and are shrinking again under upstream damming and drought. The Tigris, the Euphrates and the Karun are polluted and drawn down. In the diaspora, congregations baptise in cold northern rivers, and where the climate or the law makes that impossible they have built pools fed by running water. What counts as a iardna is now a live question inside the community rather than a philological one, and it is being settled by priests and congregations rather than by anyone writing about them. It is worth stating the shape of the problem exactly: a religion whose central rite cannot be performed without a moving river has been dispersed to places that have rivers, and has kept the rite."],terms:[{native:"ࡌࡉࡀ ࡄࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"mia hiia",gloss:"living water; flowing water, the only kind in which the rite may be performed"},{native:"ࡓࡅࡔࡌࡀ",translit:"rušma",gloss:"the sign; the marking of the forehead with water, and the name of the daily ablution before prayer"},{native:"ࡈࡀࡌࡀࡔࡀ",translit:"ṭamaša",gloss:"the threefold immersion a person performs alone, without a priest, after defilement"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡌࡁࡅࡄࡀ",translit:"mambuha",gloss:"the draught of water drunk from the priest's palm, taken with the pihta"},{native:"ࡁࡅࡓࡆࡉࡍࡒࡀ",translit:"burzinqa",gloss:"the turban of the ritual dress, into which the myrtle wreath is placed at the end of the rite"},{native:"ࡄࡀࡁࡔࡀࡁࡀ",translit:"habšaba",gloss:"the first of the week, Sunday; the ordinary day of baptism"}],texts:[{title:"Qulasta (Drower, The Canonical Prayerbook of the Mandaeans, 1959)",note:"414 prayers translated from DC 53, a manuscript copied in 1802 by the priest Adam Yuhana at Hoveyzeh; the indispensable source for the words said in the water."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)",note:"Ethnographic description of the rite as performed in the 1930s, with the ritual objects and the sequence; a record of practice in a landscape that no longer exists in the same form."},{title:"Kurt Rudolph, Die Mandäer II: Der Kult (1961)",note:"The systematic study of the ritual, setting the baptism and the death-liturgy against the textual tradition."},{title:"Šarh ḏ-Qabin ḏ-Šišlam Rba",note:"The commentary on the marriage of the Great Šišlam, published by Drower in 1950; the wedding liturgy, in which the baptismal apparatus is deployed for a different occasion."},{title:"E. S. Drower, Water into Wine (1956)",note:"Her comparative study of sacramental meals and washings in the Near East; dated in its comparative method, and still the place where the Mandaean pihta and mambuha are set beside their neighbours."}]},{slug:"masiqta",title:"The Raising",native:"ࡌࡀࡎࡉࡒࡕࡀ",translit:"nišimta",summary:"A death-liturgy that joins the soul to the spirit, carries them past the toll-houses, and is indexed with great precision to the manner in which a person died.",paragraphs:["The Mandaean person has three parts: the pagra, the body, which stays; the nišimta, the soul, which came down from the World of Light and is the reason the body can stand; and the ruha, the spirit, an intermediate faculty which is not simply a synonym for the soul and which shares its name with the queen of the lower world. At death these must be dealt with. The masiqta — the word means raising — joins soul and spirit into a single being, the ʿuṣṭuna, and brings that being up to be reunited with its dmuta, its counterpart, in Mšunia Kušṭa. It is a liturgy performed by priests on behalf of the dead, over days, at considerable expense; it is not a technique available to the person dying, and nothing in the tradition suggests that the soul's passage is a matter of what the individual knows.","The road upward is guarded. Between Tibil and the World of Light stand the maṭarata, watch-houses or toll-stations, held by uthras and by hostile powers, at which the ascending soul is stopped and examined; at the head of the road sit Abatur and his scales. A soul found short in the balance is detained. The illustrated scroll called the Diwan Abatur draws the stations one after another, and Drower published it in 1950 under a title — Progress through the Purgatories — that imported a Latin theological term the Mandaic does not use. The distinction the term smuggles in is nonetheless roughly right: these are places of detention and delay on a journey, not a final destination, and the Mandaean texts have very little of the permanent hell of their neighbours.","The rite itself is built round a meal. The zidqa brika, the blessed oblation, is prepared and eaten; small unleavened biscuits, faṭiri, are made and counted, seventy-two of them in the Ṭabahata masiqta, the liturgy of the ancestors, which is held only in the five days of Parwanaia and includes the killing of a white dove called ba. The vessels are ṭariani, plates made of mud and reed, made for the occasion. Priests recite through the hymns of the Sidra ḏ-Nišmata, the Book of Souls, which forms the core of the Qulasta, and the three books of the Left Ginza — bound so that the reader must turn the codex over and begin from the other end — carry the poetry of the soul's departure from the body. A core within the Left Ginza has been argued to be as early as the third century; the argument is contested, and belongs with the wider dispute over Mandaean datings.","The word that holds the whole institution together is laufa, connection: the bond between the living and the dead which the ritual meal maintains, and which the dukrana, the naming of the dead in the liturgy, keeps in repair. What is most striking about the corpus to a reader coming from elsewhere is the taxonomy. There are some dozens of named masiqtas, and they are indexed to the manner of death. The masiqta of Šitil is for unclean deaths, among them a priest who dies without his myrtle wreath. The masiqta of Adam is for a death on an inauspicious day. There are separate liturgies for death by burning, by falling and by drowning, for a woman who dies pregnant, and for death by snakebite. A liturgy differentiated by how a person died, rather than by who they were, is an unusual thing to find anywhere, and it follows from the logic of contamination that also sends Hibil Ziwa back into the water.","Not every masiqta is for the dead. One is performed at the ordination of a priest — the bukra, the firstborn, the new priest's first — and one at the consecration of a mandi, and their presence at those moments is not decorative: the ordinand is understood to undergo something the dead undergo. That is the strongest single piece of evidence for how the tradition understands its priesthood, and it is why the priestly scrolls and the death-liturgy are the same body of literature. The practical difficulty in the present is arithmetical. A masiqta takes days, requires several qualified priests, and cannot be shortened; the number of priests in the world is small; and communities separated by oceans cannot always assemble what the rite requires when someone dies."],terms:[{native:"ࡍࡉࡔࡉࡌࡕࡀ",translit:"nišimta",gloss:"soul; the portion brought down from the World of Light, without which the fashioned body cannot stand"},{native:"ࡏࡅࡑࡈࡅࡍࡀ",translit:"ʿuṣṭuna",gloss:"the single being into which soul and spirit are joined by the death-liturgy"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡈࡀࡓࡕࡀ",translit:"maṭarta",gloss:"watch-house, toll-station; plural maṭarata, the posts on the road between the earth and the World of Light"},{native:"ࡋࡀࡅࡐࡀ",translit:"laufa",gloss:"connection, communion; the bond between the living and the dead that the ritual meal maintains"},{native:"ࡌࡔࡅࡍࡉࡀ ࡊࡅࡔࡈࡀ",translit:"Mšunia Kušṭa",gloss:"the world of ideal counterparts, where the dmuta dwells and where the raised soul is to be rejoined to it"},{native:"ࡆࡉࡃࡒࡀ ࡁࡓࡉࡊࡀ",translit:"zidqa brika",gloss:"the blessed oblation; the offering prepared and eaten in the death-liturgy"}],texts:[{title:"Ginza Rba, Left Ginza",note:"Three books on death and the ascent, bound inverted in the same codex as the Right Ginza so that the volume must be turned over to reach them."},{title:"Diwan Abatur (DC 8)",note:"The illustrated road of the dead, station by station, with Abatur and the scales at its head; Drower's edition of 1950."},{title:"Alf Trisar Šuialia (The Thousand and Twelve Questions)",note:"Drower's translation of 1960; the priestly explanation of what the rites do, including the masiqta, in question and answer."},{title:"Šarh ḏ-Ṭabahata (the Scroll of the Ancestors)",note:"The manual for the liturgy of the ancestors held in the five days of Parwanaia, with the seventy-two faṭiri and the naming of the dead; described at length in Drower (1937)."},{title:"Qulasta, Sidra ḏ-Nišmata",note:"The Book of Souls, the core of the prayerbook, carrying the hymns sung as the soul rises; in Drower's translation of 1959."}]},{slug:"ruha-and-the-seven",title:"Ruha and the Seven",native:"ࡓࡅࡄࡀ",translit:"Ruha",summary:"The gods of Babylon survive as the seven planets, demoted to the children of the spirit — and the same community that calls them jailers keeps a book of their prognostications.",paragraphs:["Ruha is the hinge of the Mandaean lower world, and her name is the ordinary Mandaic word for the spirit in a human being. The doubleness is not an accident of vocabulary. What is intermediate in a person — the faculty between the body and the soul — is personified as the queen of the world below, mother of the seven planets and the twelve signs, and the texts occasionally give her the full title Ruha ḏ-Qudša, the Holy Spirit. That title is an inversion, and an inversion presupposes the usage it inverts; it is one of the pieces of evidence that this literature took shape within earshot of Jewish and Christian neighbours. She is not simply evil in the manner of a devil. She is turbulent, she weeps, she is at times reduced to a suppliant, and she is essential to the making of the world, working with Ptahil and against him by turns.","Her seven children carry the names of the gods of Babylon. Šamiš is the sun, Sin the moon, Nbu is Mercury, Libat is Venus — the Dilbat of the cuneiform lists, which is Ishtar — Nirig is Mars, that is Nergal, Bil is Jupiter, that is Bel, and Kiwan is Saturn. A pantheon that had been worshipped in that same landscape for three thousand years is here preserved intact under its own names and reassigned as a set of jailers. The twelve signs, the trisar, keep Aramaic names of the ordinary kind: ʿmbra the lamb for Aries, Taura the bull, Sarṭana the crab, Aria the lion, Šumbulta the ear of grain for Virgo, Arqba the scorpion, Nuna the fish. Several of them double as month-names in the Mandaean calendar, which is Babylonian in its reckoning as well as in its gods.","The tradition's attitude to these powers is genuinely double, and the doubleness sits on one shelf. The Ginza treats the planets as hostile, as the guardians of the toll-houses through which the soul must pass, and as the source of the compulsions a person is subject to. The Sfar Malwašia, the Book of the Zodiac, treats them as operative and gives the reader prognostications by sign, lunaria, omens of the year, the government of countries by planets, and predictions of weather and eclipse. Drower published it in 1949 from DC 31, an unbound quire; there is a Paris manuscript dated 1789 and a third copy, made in 1919, that was in private hands in San Diego. Nobody in the tradition seems to have thought the two attitudes needed reconciling, and the honest description is that a religion which regards the stars as a prison also keeps, and uses, the prison's timetable.","One institution ties the astrology directly to the liturgy. Every Mandaean receives a malwaša, a ritual name calculated from the zodiacal reckoning at birth, and it is that name, together with the mother's name, that is used in the baptism, in the death-liturgy and on any amulet written for the person. The name used in the street is not the name used in the rite. Identification by the mother rather than the father is the same protocol as the Aramaic incantation bowls of Sasanian Iraq follow, whoever wrote them, and it is one of the clearest points of contact between Mandaean practice and the ordinary magical practice of the region. It also means that a Mandaean's ritual identity is calculated rather than given, and calculated from the sky.","The material record of that practice is substantial. Mandaic incantation bowls, buried mouth downward under the thresholds and corners of houses, were published from Khouabir near Baghdad by Henri Pognon as early as 1898, and the Mandaic corpus has been carried forward by Edwin Yamauchi's edition of 1967 and by Christa Müller-Kessler's long labour on the material, including the Nippur bowls in the Hilprecht Collection at Jena. Beside them stand the lead rolls: incantations scratched with a pin on lead sheets, rolled and worn in a capsule on a cord, which can carry far longer texts than a bowl and continue across several sheets with catch-lines. Priests wrote amulets, and from the Islamic period onward wrote them for clients outside the community; Drower describes an iron seal-ring engraved with a lion, a scorpion, a wasp and a serpent, used in exorcism. The Ginza condemns sorcery. The same men copied the Ginza."],terms:[{native:"ࡓࡅࡄࡀ",translit:"Ruha",gloss:"spirit; both the ordinary word for the spirit in a person and the name of the queen of the lower world — the doubleness is itself the doctrine"},{native:"ࡔࡅࡁࡀ",translit:"šuba",gloss:"the seven; the planets, her children, bearing the names of the gods of Babylon"},{native:"ࡕࡓࡉࡎࡀࡓ",translit:"trisar",gloss:"the twelve; the signs of the zodiac, several of which are also the names of months"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡋࡅࡀࡔࡀ",translit:"malwaša",gloss:"the ritual name calculated from the zodiac at birth and used, with the mother's name, in every rite and on every amulet"},{native:"ࡋࡉࡁࡀࡕ",translit:"Libat",gloss:"Venus; the Dilbat of the Babylonian lists, which is Ishtar, kept under her own name and reassigned"},{native:"ࡕࡉࡁࡉࡋ",translit:"Tibil",gloss:"the earth; the middle world, between the light above and the darkness below"}],texts:[{title:"Sfar Malwašia (Drower, The Book of the Zodiac, 1949)",note:"Twenty books of astrological material from DC 31: signs, lunaria, omens of the year, the government of countries, predictions of weather and eclipse."},{title:"Ginza Rba, Right Ginza",note:"The counter-position: the planets as the guardians of the toll-houses and the source of compulsion, in the same community's principal scripture."},{title:"Edwin M. Yamauchi, Mandaic Incantation Texts (American Oriental Series 49, 1967)",note:"The standard edition of a body of Mandaic bowls, with a full apparatus on the demons named."},{title:"Henri Pognon, Inscriptions mandaïtes des coupes de Khouabir (1898)",note:"The first substantial publication of Mandaic bowls, from a site near Baghdad; earlier than Montgomery's Nippur volume and the beginning of the whole field."},{title:"Christa Müller-Kessler, Die Zauberschalentexte in der Hilprecht-Sammlung, Jena (2005)",note:"Nippur bowls from a collection with an excavation history, edited by the scholar who has done most for the Mandaic and Syriac magical material."}]},{slug:"the-priesthood",title:"The Priesthood",native:"ࡍࡀࡑࡅࡓࡀࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"naṣuraiia",summary:"Three grades, a reed hut, sixty days of seclusion, an enormous quantity of text to be held in the head — and a chain of copyists that is now the most contested evidence in the field.",paragraphs:["The community has two names for itself and the difference between them organises its literature. Mandaiia, the knowers, from manda, knowledge, is the ordinary word for Mandaeans at large. Naṣuraiia — from a root meaning to observe or to guard — is used of the initiated and of the priestly knowledge, and it is the word that stands behind the term Nasoraean in the scholarly literature. The distinction is practical: the Ginza and the Qulasta are for everyone, while the diwans, the illustrated priestly scrolls and the commentaries on them, are naṣuraiia texts whose circulation is restricted. A reader outside the community holding a translation of Alma Rišaia Rba is holding something that was not intended to travel, and that fact is part of the history of the field rather than a footnote to it.","There are three ordained grades and one office below them. The šganda is a ritual assistant, not ordained, who serves the priest; many priests have served as šganda in youth. The šualia is the novice under instruction. The tarmida is the priest — the word means disciple. The ganzibra is the senior priest, and the title is Old Persian, ganza-bara, treasurer, an Iranian loanword sitting in the name of a Mandaean office and quietly testifying to where the institution took shape. The rišama is the head of the people. Ordination is not hereditary in principle, though in practice priestly families recur. The rite is conducted in an andiruna, a reed hut built for the purpose and dismantled afterwards; the initiating priest and the novice remain inside for a week without sleeping, and the required scrolls must be physically present in the hut or the ceremony is void. Sixty days of seclusion at home follow, and then the new priest's first death-liturgy, the bukra.","What actually limits ordination is memory. A tarmida must know the prayerbook and the ritual sequences by heart, and the quantity is very large; the apprenticeship is measured in years, and it cannot be compressed. The community itself names this, rather than any political pressure, as the constraint that will decide its future, because a priest can only be made by priests and the arithmetic is unforgiving. The most recent figure to circulate widely is the estimate of Rišama Salah Choheili in 2016, which put the number of Mandaean priests in the world at about forty-three or forty-four — thirteen in Australia, twelve in Iraq, six in Iran, a handful each in Sweden, the United States, Jordan, the Netherlands, Turkey and Denmark — with perhaps sixteen šgandas who might in time be ordained.","This has happened before, and the community's recovery from it is documented in its own hand. In 1831 an outbreak of cholera at Shushtar killed, so far as anyone can establish, every Mandaean priest then living. Two learned laymen — yalufia, men who knew the texts without being ordained — Yahia Bihram and his kinsman Ram Zihrun, initiated each other as tarmidas at Suq eš-Šuyuk in Iraq, and afterwards as ganzibras, and rebuilt the priesthood from that point. They also copied manuscripts, steadily, for decades; at least six of Yahia Bihram's copies are in the Drower Collection at the Bodleian today. The episode is known chiefly because they recorded it in their colophons, which is to say that a community documented its own near-extinction in the margins of the books it was saving.","Those colophons are now the central evidentiary battleground of the field. Mandaean manuscripts carry chains of copyists, sometimes very long, naming who copied from whom; Jorunn Buckley's Great Stem of Souls turned them into usable historical material, tracing lineages, recovering the names of women copyists, and reaching back to a scribe named Zazai ḏ-Gawazta, who appears as the first copyist in the chains of the Qulasta, the Thousand and Twelve Questions, the Scroll of Exalted Kingship and others, and whom she places in the third century as a contemporary of Bahram I, who reigned from 271 to 274. Against this Kevin van Bladel has argued that a key colophon phrase in the prayerbook, long read as a memory of the Arab conquest, is in fact a standard formula for dating by the Islamic calendar, which removes the anchor holding the chain in the third century. James McGrath and others have contested that reading. The dispute is unresolved, and it decides how old this religion is."],terms:[{native:"ࡍࡀࡑࡅࡓࡀࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"naṣuraiia",gloss:"the observers or guardians; used of the initiated and of the restricted priestly knowledge, and the word behind scholarly 'Nasoraean'"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡍࡃࡀࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"mandaiia",gloss:"the knowers; the ordinary name of the community, from manda, knowledge"},{native:"ࡔࡅࡀࡋࡉࡀ",translit:"šualia",gloss:"the novice under instruction for the priesthood, who is enclosed in the hut with his initiator"},{native:"ࡔࡂࡀࡍࡃࡀ",translit:"šganda",gloss:"the ritual assistant, unordained, who serves the priest and is often ordained later"},{native:"ࡀࡍࡃࡉࡓࡅࡍࡀ",translit:"andiruna",gloss:"the reed hut built for an ordination or a wedding and taken down when it is done"},{native:"ࡔࡉࡔࡋࡀࡌ ࡓࡁࡀ",translit:"Šišlam Rba",gloss:"the Great Šišlam, archetypal priest and bridegroom, in whose person the ordinand is invested"}],texts:[{title:"E. S. Drower, The Coronation of the Great Šišlam (1962)",note:"The ordination liturgy with its commentary; the fullest account in any language of how a Mandaean priest is made."},{title:"Diwan Malkuta ʿLaita (The Scroll of Exalted Kingship)",note:"A priestly scroll required to be present in the initiation hut; translated by Jorunn Jacobsen Buckley (1993)."},{title:"Jorunn Jacobsen Buckley, The Great Stem of Souls: Reconstructing Mandaean History (Gorgias Press)",note:"The study that turned the colophons into historical evidence — scribal lineages, named women copyists, and the case for a third-century Zazai ḏ-Gawazta."},{title:"Kevin T. van Bladel, From Sasanian Mandaeans to Ṣābians of the Marshes (Brill, 2017)",note:"The counter-case: a rereading of the prayerbook colophon that removes the third-century anchor, and an argument for a fifth-century Mesopotamian origin."},{title:"Alma Rišaia Zuṭa",note:"The lesser of the two commentaries Drower published together in 1963; a naṣuraiia text on the priest and his correspondences, restricted in circulation within the community."}]},{slug:"origins",title:"The Question of Origins",native:"ࡄࡀࡓࡀࡍ ࡂࡀࡅࡀࡉࡕࡀ",translit:"Haran Gawaita",summary:"The community's own texts place its beginning in first-century Palestine; every artefact, loanword and letter-form we can actually date is Mesopotamian. Both accounts are set out here.",paragraphs:["The tradition's own account is in the Haran Gawaita, the Inner Haran, which Drower published in 1953 from DC 9. It opens with a migration: sixty thousand Nasoraeans abandoning what it calls the Sign of the Seven and leaving Jerusalem for the Inner Haran, called the mountains of Madai, where a king named Ardban — identified in Mandaean chronicle material as an Artabanus, and so a Parthian — receives them. The text puts the flight before the destruction of the Temple and attributes it to persecution by Jewish authorities. On this account the religion is a first-century movement of the Jordan valley, driven east, and everything Mesopotamian about it is exile rather than origin. The scroll is fragmentary, difficult, and in places barely construable, and Drower said so.","There is real internal evidence pointing west, and it should not be dismissed because the scroll is hard. The word for a river is iardna, a Jordan — a common noun whose sense presupposes one particular river a long way from Iraq. John the Baptist is the great baptiser and the honoured teacher, and no other surviving community holds that position. The polemic is aimed with unusual precision at Jerusalem and at the Temple. Ruha carries the title Ruha ḏ-Qudša, the Holy Spirit, which parodies a usage that had to exist first. The name naṣuraiia sits beside Nazorean in a way that has occupied scholars for a century. Rudolf Macuch argued for western features in the language; Kurt Rudolph's synthesis, which remained the field's default for decades, made the Mandaeans a heterodox Jewish baptising movement of the Jordan valley displaced eastward in the first or second century.","Everything that can be dated, however, is Mesopotamian. The earliest Mandaic writing is on metal and clay: the lead rolls, dated to the third to fifth centuries, and the incantation bowls of Sasanian and early Islamic Iraq. The script belongs, on Häberl's analysis, with Elymaic and Characenean, the Parthian-period chancery hands of southern Mesopotamia and Khuzestan. The ritual vocabulary is full of Iranian loans, ganzibra and drabša among them, sitting in the names of the central office and the central object. The months are Babylonian and the planets are the Babylonian gods under their own names. And the Haran Gawaita as we have it refers repeatedly to Baghdad, a city founded in 762, so that the text carrying the tradition's claim to a first-century Palestinian origin cannot in its present form be earlier than the late eighth century.","Van Bladel's 2017 study pressed this into a hard thesis: that Mandaeism arose in Sasanian Mesopotamia no earlier than the fifth century, that no source places it anywhere else, and that the colophon chains do not reach back as far as has been claimed. His decisive move is philological — the rereading of the prayerbook colophon phrase discussed in the previous current — and it was met by Buckley's colophon work and by James McGrath's rejoinder. The earliest external notice usually cited is the Scholion of the East Syriac writer Theodore bar Konai, composed near the end of the eighth century, which describes the group and quotes Mandaic. Before that there is nothing certain from outside, and the argument turns entirely on how much weight internal evidence can carry.","One further body of evidence sits between the two positions without settling either. Ibn al-Nadim, writing the Fihrist at Baghdad in 987 or 988, describes the Mughtasila, the washers, a baptising community of the marshes of southern Iraq whose founder he calls al-Ḥasīḥ, that is Elchasai, and among whom the father of Mani lived — a report the Cologne Mani Codex independently supports from the other side, in Greek. Whether the Mughtasila are the Mandaeans, their neighbours, their ancestors or their rivals has never been resolved. What can be said is that southern Mesopotamia in late antiquity held more than one community that washed in rivers, and that the Mandaeans are the one that survived. Under Islam they were identified with the Ṣābiʾūn, the Sabians named three times in the Qur'an and nowhere defined there, and it is under that name that they held a protected legal status for most of the following thirteen centuries. A last point of manners: the community's account of where it comes from is a claim about who its members are, and not only a hypothesis for outsiders to score."],terms:[{native:"ࡌࡀࡍࡃࡀ",translit:"manda",gloss:"knowledge; the word the community is named from, and the second element of Manda ḏ-Hiia"},{native:"ࡄࡀࡓࡀࡍ ࡂࡀࡅࡀࡉࡕࡀ",translit:"Haran Gawaita",gloss:"the Inner Haran; the scroll carrying the tradition's own account of its migration out of Jerusalem"},{native:"ࡀࡓࡃࡁࡀࡍ",translit:"Ardban",gloss:"Artabanus; the king said in the scroll to have received the migrants — a Parthian, and so a first- or second-century setting"},{native:"ࡈࡅࡓࡀ ࡖࡌࡀࡃࡀࡉ",translit:"Tura ḏ-Madai",gloss:"the mountain of the Medes; the destination of the migration, whose identification with Media is disputed"},{native:"ࡌࡉࡓࡉࡀࡉ",translit:"Miriai",gloss:"the girl of a Jewish family in the Book of John who leaves her people for the new teaching; the tradition's own image of its separation"},{native:"ࡓࡅࡄࡀ ࡖࡒࡅࡃࡔࡀ",translit:"Ruha ḏ-Qudša",gloss:"the Holy Spirit; here a title of the queen of the lower world — an inversion that presupposes the usage it inverts"}],texts:[{title:"E. S. Drower, The Haran Gawaita and the Baptism of Hibil-Ziwa (1953)",note:"Two scrolls in one volume, from DC 9 and DC 35; the first carries the migration narrative on which the tradition's claim to a Palestinian origin rests."},{title:"Kurt Rudolph, Die Mandäer I: Prolegomena — Das Mandäerproblem (1960)",note:"The classic statement of the western case: a heterodox Jewish baptising movement of the Jordan valley, displaced eastward."},{title:"Charles G. Häberl, 'Iranian Scripts for Aramaic Languages', BASOR 341 (2006)",note:"The script as evidence: Mandaic among the Parthian-era chancery hands of southern Mesopotamia and Khuzestan."},{title:"Kevin T. van Bladel, From Sasanian Mandaeans to Ṣābians of the Marshes (2017)",note:"The strong Mesopotamian case, dating the religion no earlier than the fifth century; reviewed critically by James McGrath and others."},{title:"Ibn al-Nadim, Kitāb al-Fihrist (completed at Baghdad, 987/8)",note:"The notice of the Mughtasila, the washers of the marshes, their founder al-Ḥasīḥ, and Mani's father among them; corroborated from the Greek side by the Cologne Mani Codex."}]},{slug:"the-archive",title:"The Archive",native:"ࡄࡉࡉࡀ ࡆࡀࡊࡉࡍ",translit:"Hiia zakin",summary:"How the books left the marshes, what a British judge's wife did with them, the New Testament controversy they were dragged into, and who edits them now.",paragraphs:["Europe met the Mandaeans in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries at Basra, in the persons of Portuguese and Carmelite missionaries who called them the Christians of St John — the repeated baptism read as a garbled sacrament — and who ministered to them on that footing. The Carmelite Ignatius a Jesu published an account of their rites and errors at Rome in 1652. Manuscripts followed, into Paris and Oxford. Matthias Norberg printed a Ginza in 1815 and 1816, transcribed into Syriac letters, under the title Codex Nasaraeus, Liber Adami appellatus — the Book of Adam, as Europe then called it — and the transcription was bad enough to be nearly useless. Julius Heinrich Petermann's lithographic edition of the Mandaic text followed in 1867, and the field properly begins there; N. Siouffi, French vice-consul at Mosul, published an account from living informants in 1880, and Wilhelm Brandt's study of the religion appeared in 1889.","Then came the Mandäerfrage. In the 1920s Richard Reitzenstein, and after him Rudolf Bultmann, used the Mandaean books to reconstruct a pre-Christian Gnostic redeemer myth lying behind the Gospel of John — a hypothesis that made a small community in the Iraqi marshes briefly central to New Testament scholarship, on the strength of manuscripts none of which was older than the seventeenth century. Hans Lietzmann and F. C. Burkitt attacked the dating; the reconstruction did not survive; and the collateral damage was considerable, since for a generation afterwards Mandaean studies carried the odour of a discredited argument that had never been the Mandaeans' own. The episode is worth remembering as a case of what happens when a living tradition is conscripted as evidence in somebody else's dispute.","Ethel Stefana Drower, born Stevens in 1879, went to Iraq in 1921 when her husband was appointed adviser to the ministry of justice, and stayed twenty-six years. She attended the rites, learned the language, and — the decisive act — had manuscripts copied for her, principally through the priest Sheikh Negm bar Zahroon, who became her chief informant. The result is the Drower Collection at the Bodleian: manuscripts 1 to 53 given in 1958, 54 in 1961, and 55, her own notebook, added in 1986. From it came the Prayerbook, the Thousand and Twelve Questions, the Haran Gawaita, the Diwan Abatur, the Book of the Zodiac, the Coronation of the Great Šišlam, and the two commentaries. The legacy is genuinely ambivalent and should be stated as such: much of what she published is naṣuraiia material that priests were not supposed to release, and some of it now survives only because she paid for copies to be made. Both of those sentences are true at once, and opinion within the community is not uniform about them.","The scholarship since has been cumulative. Drower and Rudolf Macuch produced A Mandaic Dictionary in 1963; Macuch's Handbook of Classical and Modern Mandaic followed in 1965; Rudolph's two volumes gave the doctrine and the cult their standard treatment. Buckley worked through the colophons and published Drower's scholarly correspondence in 2012. Charles Häberl wrote the grammar of the Neo-Mandaic of Khorramshahr in 2009, translated the last tractate of the Right Ginza as The Book of Kings and the Explanations of This World in 2022, and with James McGrath produced the critical Book of John in 2020, prepared in consultation with Mandaeans and published open access. The community's own editors now do a large share of the work: Majid Fandi Al-Mubaraki's typeset Mandaic texts, Carlos Gelbert's complete English Ginza of 2011, the translation of Qais Al-Saadi endorsed by the community's religious leadership, and the scholarship of Brikha Nasoraia, who is both a priest and an academic. The archive is no longer only in Oxford.","The present has to close this hall, because it is not settled. Estimates of the Iraqi community before 2003 run from sixty to seventy-five thousand; by 2007 the figure commonly given had fallen to about five thousand, and a 2019 survey put it near three thousand. Roughly nine in ten Iraqi Mandaeans were killed or left. Goldsmithing had long been a Mandaean trade, which made families visible targets for kidnapping for ransom in the years after the invasion, and the Islamic State later gave those in its path the choice of conversion or flight. There are now congregations in Sweden, Australia, the United States, the Netherlands, Germany and Jordan, and the global figure is usually given as sixty to a hundred thousand. In Iran the difficulty is legal standing rather than violence. Neo-Mandaic, which its speakers call Raṭna, has a few hundred speakers, mostly from Ahvaz and Khorramshahr, and it is the only living language descended directly from a classical literary Aramaic — the dialect and the liturgy are the same tongue, which is not true anywhere else in this Scriptorium. Mandaean texts end with a formula: uHiia zakin, and Life is victorious. It is written at the foot of colophons by copyists who in several documented cases were writing during an epidemic, a flight or a war."],terms:[{native:"ࡄࡉࡉࡀ ࡆࡀࡊࡉࡍ",translit:"Hiia zakin",gloss:"the Life is victorious; the formula with which Mandaean texts and colophons close"},{native:"ࡊࡅࡓࡀࡎࡀ",translit:"kurasa",gloss:"an unbound quire; the physical form in which several of the Drower manuscripts, including the Book of the Zodiac, reached Oxford"},{native:"ࡔࡀࡐࡕࡀ",translit:"šapta",gloss:"a scroll, particularly a charm scroll — the format of the protective and exorcistic literature, as against the codex of the Ginza"},{native:"ࡃࡅࡊࡓࡀࡍࡀ",translit:"dukrana",gloss:"commemoration; the naming of the dead in the liturgy, and the same impulse that fills the colophons with the names of copyists"},{native:"ࡓࡀࡈࡍࡀ",translit:"Raṭna",gloss:"the name its speakers give Neo-Mandaic; a few hundred speakers, chiefly from Ahvaz and Khorramshahr"},{native:"ࡀࡎࡅࡕࡀ",translit:"asuta",gloss:"healing; the ordinary Mandaean greeting and blessing, and one of the commonest words in the prayers"}],texts:[{title:"Matthias Norberg, Codex Nasaraeus, Liber Adami appellatus (1815–16)",note:"The first printed Ginza, transcribed into Syriac letters and close to unusable; a record of how badly Europe first read this literature."},{title:"J. H. Petermann, Thesaurus s. Liber Magnus, vulgo 'Liber Adami' appellatus (1867)",note:"The lithographic edition of the Mandaic text from four Paris manuscripts, on which serious study of the Ginza begins."},{title:"Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das grosse Buch der Mandäer (1925)",note:"The standard German translation of Right and Left Ginza; a century old and still the version most scholarship works from."},{title:"E. S. Drower and Rudolf Macuch, A Mandaic Dictionary (1963)",note:"The lexicon of the whole tradition, built from the manuscripts Drower had copied; the tool that made everything after it possible."},{title:"Charles G. Häberl, The Neo-Mandaic Dialect of Khorramshahr (2009)",note:"The descriptive grammar of the living language, recorded from speakers of a dialect whose home city was destroyed in the Iran–Iraq war."}]}]};export{e as default};
